environment", "Consistent polling showing Becerra ahead by 17-22 points, including recent SurveyUSA and PPIC surveys", "All six expert ratings place the race as Solid or Safe Democratic", "Prediction markets price Becerra at 95-96 percent"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Becerra leading by double digits, with the newest surveys widening his lead, and raters and markets uniformly treat the race as safely Democratic. The fundamentals point to a smaller margin than polls suggest, so a modest haircut from the polling average is warranted, but nothing indicates a competitive race.", "watch": "A late external shock or a major Becerra misstep on affordability could tighten the race, though Hilton would need a historic swing to contend.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Gavin Newsom (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's open governor's seat strongly favors Democrat Xavier Becerra over Republican Steve Hilton. The state's 12-point Democratic baseline, combined with a favorable national environment, gives Becerra a substantial structural edge even without an incumbent on the ballot. Polling has been consistent all cycle: recent SurveyUSA and PPIC surveys show leads of 17 to 22 points, and earlier polls never had the race inside 21.\n\nOutside assessments agree. All six expert raters call the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Becerra's chances at 95 percent or higher. Hilton has campaigned aggressively on cost of living and government dysfunction, including pledges to abolish the DMV, and has slightly outraised Becerra, but no evidence suggests he has closed the gap. Becerra's low-profile strategy has not hurt him in surveys. The race remains safely Democratic, with the main uncertainty being the final margin rather than the winner.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 17.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}]}